{"authored" [{"question" "q-161286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\,u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ Use the scalar surface-area factor $\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|$ with its absolute values for the stated parameter domain. What is the missing expression?" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/review.json" "file_sha256" "06b4035579f7bcee6392cc848de8dd96b9c5ace70f3095ca1a2d219c5a746e27" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "195a12a1740de62fe140f5fef35c0a695dbea24d058a8beb153f6745f45e7dbe" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/verification.json" "basis" 2164 "rationale" "The Euclidean norm requires an absolute value of the parameter. Keep the original general domain, correct its area factor and derivation, and add the absolute-value answer with a new field version, retaining the old field, choices, key and history. q161286 uses sqrt8|v|; q161134 uses sqrt8|u|."} {"question" "q-161286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a scalar surface integral, the pullback integrand is $f(r(u,v))\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|$. The derivatives and cross product are\n\n$$r_u=(1,-2u,1),\\qquad r_v=(0,2v,0),\\qquad r_u\\times r_v=(-2v,0,2v).$$\n\nThus the nonnegative area factor is\n\n$$\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|=\\sqrt{(-2v)^2+(2v)^2}=\\sqrt8\\,|v|.$$\n\nSubstitution into the scalar function gives\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=-u-(v^2-u^2)-u=u^2-v^2-2u.$$\n\nThe missing integrand for the stated general parameter domain is therefore\n\n$$(u^2-v^2-2u)\\sqrt8\\,|v|.$$\n\nThe source omitted the absolute value and supplied no sign restriction on $v$. Its option $(u^2-v^2-2u)\\sqrt8\\,v$ is valid on a positive-$v$ patch, but fails when $v<0$: at $(u,v)=(1,-1)$ the corrected value is $-2\\sqrt8$, while that source option gives $2\\sqrt8$. The capture used a distinct explicit $v>0$, regular, one-to-one recovery request. This study question retains the original domain and supplies the correct absolute-value choice.\n\nThe parametrization loses rank at $v=0$. As usual, interpreting the displayed equality as an integral over the surface assumes regular patches and no multiple coverage except on negligible boundaries; the local area factor remains $\\sqrt8|v|$." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/review.json" "file_sha256" "06b4035579f7bcee6392cc848de8dd96b9c5ace70f3095ca1a2d219c5a746e27" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "195a12a1740de62fe140f5fef35c0a695dbea24d058a8beb153f6745f45e7dbe" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/verification.json" "basis" 2164 "rationale" "The Euclidean norm requires an absolute value of the parameter. Keep the original general domain, correct its area factor and derivation, and add the absolute-value answer with a new field version, retaining the old field, choices, key and history. q161286 uses sqrt8|v|; q161134 uses sqrt8|u|."} {"question" "q-161286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}|v|"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/review.json" "file_sha256" "06b4035579f7bcee6392cc848de8dd96b9c5ace70f3095ca1a2d219c5a746e27" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "195a12a1740de62fe140f5fef35c0a695dbea24d058a8beb153f6745f45e7dbe" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161286/verification.json" "basis" 2164 "rationale" "The Euclidean norm requires an absolute value of the parameter. Keep the original general domain, correct its area factor and derivation, and add the absolute-value answer with a new field version, retaining the old field, choices, key and history. q161286 uses sqrt8|v|; q161134 uses sqrt8|u|."} {"question" "q-161286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\,u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065891/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdfa6a803b0f5b4dd40bc4cb5a6a37fd1c18de08705f0016275ee683974ae5bf" "basis" 2162 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,0,\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\,0,\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065891/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdfa6a803b0f5b4dd40bc4cb5a6a37fd1c18de08705f0016275ee683974ae5bf" "basis" 2162 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b20e1f45a42d5fede32fa998c7066f7dd3766d8c34d0223df22b1ac471cd7d6e" "basis" 2162 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/